    "summary": "Killer Garcia tones, rare Big Boy Pete, and a soaring The Eleven",
    "blurb": "The show delivers crushing jams on Alligator, The Eleven and a fiery transition into Good Lovin'. Rare cuts like Big Boy Pete and Mason's Children sit alongside classic folk‑rock moments such as Seasons Of My Heart. Overall the performance is tight, high‑energy and captured in crystal‑clear soundboard form.",
    "review": "New Year's Eve 1969 at Boston’s Tea Party stands out as one of the band’s most electrifying late‑sixties outings. Jerry Garcia rides a “killer” lead tone that shines on China Cat Sunflower, Alligator and the soaring solo during The Eleven, while his vocal delivery on High Time is described as cool and powerful. Phil Lesh locks into the rhythmic vortex that launches The Eleven and trades duels with Jerry that keep the low end bubbling. Bob Weir’s honest, sweet vocals on Mama Tried and Dire Wolf add a grounded folk feel, even if his guitar sometimes recedes in the mix. The rhythm section—Mickey Hart’s cannon‑like fills (despite a missing tom mic) and Bill Kreutzmann’s steady drive—propels the high‑octane jams that define the second set. Rare gems like Big Boy Pete, Mason's Children and the seamless Good Lovin' → The Eleven transition make this NYE marathon a must‑hear snapshot of the Dead at the brink of their psychedelic zenith.",
